{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And he called it Novach after his name.</b> The heh is weak (rapheh). And it says in the Midrash of Ruth: because that name did not endure for him — that is, as if it is written without an א — and like it is ״לבנות לה בית בארץ שנער״ (Zechariah 5:11), with a weak לה, for the prophet asked (Zechariah 5:11), ״אנה המה מוליכות את האיפה״, and Targum Yonatan rendered: 'to where they are exiling the people who marry and give in false measures'; and the angel answered him, ״לבנות לה בית בארץ שנער״, and this was said concerning some of Judah and Benjamin who lingered in Babylonia and did not wish to go up to Jerusalem even after the building of the House had begun, and therefore ״לבנות לה״ was not written with a מפיק (i.e., the heh lacks a mappiq), because there is no permanence to falsehood, and no salvation for one who does not walk in the way of HaShem. And so King Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 12:19): ״שפת אמת תכון לעד ועד ארגיעה לשון שקר״ — he means that things which are truth endure forever, but falsehood has no permanence except for a moment, provisionally, for it is immediately tested and found to be falsehood; and therefore a person is obligated to hate falsehood and to love truth, in the manner that the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Zechariah 8:19): ״והאמת והשלום אהבו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The prooftext from Zechariah 5:11 is cited twice for two different phrases; the reference in the heading is to the full verse. The phrase אנה המה מוליכות את האיפה is from Zechariah 5:10–11; he attributes the question to the prophet rather than the angel who speaks it in the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:36.323715207Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}